# THE LIMITS OF HUMAN COORDINATION
## A Formal Theory of Civilizational Coordination Capacity, Scaling Constraints, and AI-Mediated Transition to a Multiplanetary Order

**A Dissertation Submitted in Partial Fulfillment of the Requirements for the Degree of Doctor of Philosophy**

**Fields:** Complex Systems Theory · Collective Intelligence · Civilizational Studies · Information Theory

---

## ABSTRACT

Human civilization is best understood not as an accumulation of technologies or resources, but as a sequence of phase transitions in coordination capacity — the measurable ability of large populations to organize collective action under conditions of uncertainty, delay, and incentive misalignment. This dissertation develops the first unified formal theory of civilizational coordination capacity (CCC), grounded in information theory, game theory, and network science. We define coordination capacity rigorously as a function of population size, communication bandwidth, institutional trust, and incentive alignment, and prove a set of Coordination Scaling Theorems establishing fundamental upper bounds on coherent collective action at each scale of organization. We demonstrate that hierarchical depth must grow logarithmically with population size — formally, $L = \log_k N$ — in order to prevent coordination overhead from diverging [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.14325) , and that this logarithmic scaling law implies a series of predictable coordination phase transitions throughout history. We further show that many of our species' evolved social behaviors and culturally maintained social technologies emerged specifically as solutions to the coordination problem imposed by group size [nih](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10427830/) , and that each major civilizational technology — language, writing, institutions, science, digital networks — constitutes an engineering solution to a coordination bottleneck, not merely an intellectual achievement. We then formalize the role of artificial intelligence as a potential coordination infrastructure capable of operating above human cognitive limits, modeling it as a channel-capacity amplifier in the Shannon-theoretic sense. Finally, we extend our framework to the multiplanetary case, formalizing the Autonomous Aligned Coordination (AAC) problem under signal propagation delay and proving that, beyond a critical latency threshold, centralized coordination becomes thermodynamically infeasible. We conclude with a rigorous research program of eight open problems constituting the frontier of coordination science.

