## CHAPTER 7 — TOWARD AN EMPIRICAL SCIENCE OF COORDINATION LIMITS

### 7.1 Measurable Predictions

The foregoing formal framework generates a set of empirically falsifiable predictions. We state these as open problems constituting the research frontier:

**Open Problem 1 (Population-CCC Curve).** Measure the empirical relationship between organizational size $N$ and per-agent coordination output (in bits of jointly aligned action per unit time) across organizations ranging from 5-person teams to nation-states. Our theory predicts $\mathcal{C}/N \sim N^{-0.5}/\log N$.

**Open Problem 2 (Trust Decay Constant).** Measure the empirical trust decay constant $\bar{w}$ in large-scale social networks to calibrate Theorem 3.4 and predict institutional failure thresholds.

**Open Problem 3 (AI Amplification Ratio).** Empirically measure the coordination amplification ratio $\mathcal{C}^{\mathcal{M}}/\mathcal{C}$ for AI-assisted teams versus unassisted teams at matched scales. Work developing effective applied AI for national-scale healthcare systems, where data sensitivity and outcome criticality make this a rigorous testing domain, provides an ideal starting point for developing governance tools and measuring coordination improvement. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.06211)

**Open Problem 4 (Bifurcation Detection).** Develop real-time metrics for detecting incipient Coordination Bifurcation (Definition 5.3) in geopolitical AI deployments — measuring inter-bloc $\Omega$ as a warning indicator.

**Open Problem 5 (AAC Protocol Design).** Design and formally verify a concrete Autonomous Aligned Coordination protocol satisfying the conditions of Theorem 6.2, implementable in Mars-analog environments on Earth (Antarctic stations, submarine deployments).

**Open Problem 6 (Value Compression Complexity).** Determine the minimum description length $K(\hat{\mathcal{V}})$ for a value structure $\mathcal{V}$ capable of guiding autonomous agents in the AAC setting. Is there a finite $K(\hat{\mathcal{V}})$ sufficient for civilizational-scale coordination?

**Open Problem 7 (Coordination Entropy Measurement).** Develop practical measurement instruments for $H_{\text{coord}}(\mathcal{A})$ — the coordination entropy of a social system — and test the prediction that civilizational resilience correlates with $H_{\text{coord}}$.

**Open Problem 8 (Phase Transition Signatures).** Identify the empirical signatures of an imminent coordination phase transition — the analog, for coordination systems, of the order-parameter fluctuations that precede physical phase transitions. What early warning signals preceded the writing revolution, the institutional revolution, the scientific revolution?

### 7.2 Methodological Toolkit

The empirical science of coordination limits requires an interdisciplinary methodology combining:

- **Computational Social Science:** Large-scale behavioral data from organizational databases, digital communication logs, and experimental platforms.
- **Information-Theoretic Estimation:** Techniques for estimating mutual information, channel capacity, and KL divergence in real social data.
- **Agent-Based Simulation:** Agent-based models of group coordination toward behavioral synchrony can spell out the mechanics of group size limits and explain why it is cognitively demanding for humans to live in the large societies we actually inhabit. [nih](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10427830/)
- **Institutional Analysis:** Historical comparative analysis of coordination transitions, using the framework of Section 4 to code historical data.
- **Experimental Governance:** Mars-analog governance experiments in isolated communities, testing AAC protocol candidates.

